%!testlib
%!
% What the command line confines a program to, run by
% run-cli-sandbox-test.sh with no --no-sandbox: prints SUCCESS iff every
% assertion holds.
%
% Every other PostScript test in this suite is run with --no-sandbox,
% which is right for them and leaves the shipped configuration -- the one
% every ordinary run has -- checked by nothing from the language side.
% This is that check. It is deliberately about what the command line
% arranges rather than about the mechanism underneath, which the C-level
% sandbox tests hold: which directories a run may reach, and whether the
% operators that decide that are still there to be named.
%
% OUTSIDE names a file the run was not given, in a directory that is
% neither the current one nor the temporary one nor the program's own.


% What a procedure raised, with whatever it left behind taken off the
% stack. The depth remembered is the one below the procedure: the count
% at this point includes both the procedure and the name just pushed for
% it, and `stopped` consumes the procedure, so a case that leaves nothing
% would otherwise be asked to pop a negative number of operands.

% The operators that configure the confinement are gone by the time a
% program runs. Enforcement does not rest on their absence -- the checks
% below hold either way -- so this is the second line, and it is here
% because it is the half that goes missing without a sound.
systemdict /.permitfileread known not
    (.permitfileread is not in systemdict) assert
systemdict /.permitfilewrite known not
    (.permitfilewrite is not in systemdict) assert
systemdict /.lockdown known not
    (.lockdown is not in systemdict) assert
systemdict /.resourcefileopen known not
    (.resourcefileopen is not in systemdict) assert

% and naming one is undefined rather than reaching something else
{ (/) .permitfileread } outcome /undefined eq
    (naming .permitfileread is undefined) assert

% the working directory is the program's to read and to write
{ (cli_sandbox_probe.txt) (w) file dup (data) writestring closefile } outcome
    /ok eq (a file in the current directory is written) assert
{ (cli_sandbox_probe.txt) (r) file dup closefile } outcome
    /ok eq (a file in the current directory is read) assert
{ (cli_sandbox_probe.txt) deletefile } outcome
    /ok eq (a file in the current directory is deleted) assert

% and what it was not given is refused, whichever way it is asked for
{ OUTSIDE (r) file } outcome /invalidfileaccess eq
    (a file outside is not read) assert
{ OUTSIDE (w) file } outcome /invalidfileaccess eq
    (a file outside is not written) assert
{ OUTSIDE deletefile } outcome /invalidfileaccess eq
    (a file outside is not deleted) assert
OUTSIDE status not
    (a file outside does not answer status) assert

% The temporary directory is one of the places that is not given. It
% reads like part of a working area and was granted here once; it is
% shared with every account on the machine, and the interpreter's own
% scratch does not reach it through the permitted set, so a run has no
% reason to hand it to a program it does not trust. SCRATCH names a file
% in it that the harness made, so a pass here is the refusal and not the
% file being absent -- which is why the read is checked as well as the
% write.
{ SCRATCH (r) file } outcome /invalidfileaccess eq
    (a file in the temporary directory is not read) assert
{ SCRATCH (w) file } outcome /invalidfileaccess eq
    (a file in the temporary directory is not written) assert
{ TMPNEW (w) file } outcome /invalidfileaccess eq
    (a new file in the temporary directory is not created) assert

% Naming an output file grants that file, not the directory holding it.
% The two are told apart by putting the output somewhere other than where
% the run stands: were they the same place the working-directory permit
% would cover everything and this would prove nothing. A name carrying a
% %d is the exception and still takes the directory, because which pages
% there will be is not known until the program has run.
{ OUTSIB (r) file } outcome /invalidfileaccess eq
    (a file beside the output is not read) assert
{ OUTSIB (w) file } outcome /invalidfileaccess eq
    (a file beside the output is not written) assert
{ OUTNEW (w) file } outcome /invalidfileaccess eq
    (a new file beside the output is not created) assert

0 0 moveto 10 10 lineto stroke showpage

verdict
